  "about": "One PoolAverageFeed per asset on Robinhood Chain mainnet, read by script/DeployPoolFeeds.s.sol. pool is the asset's Uniswap v3 pool against quoteToken at fee, and the script holds it to the v3 factory's own answer. minLiquidity is the least liquidity in range, in the pool's own units, as a decimal string: here half of what each pool held in range on Friday 2026-10-09 at 16:30 UTC (block 84298619), rounded down to two figures; a person sets it again before deploying, from two weeks of the dry run's meanLiquidity inside the session: about half its 5th percentile. jumpBps is how far Chainlink's latest answer may be from its own average over the window. holds says why the design keeps an asset owner-signed for now: feed-from-pools (the Chainlink feed is itself made from pools), rounds-unread (the feed's round history has not been read), person-decides. The script deploys no feed for an asset with a hold unless accepted is true, which a person sets after deciding. Whatever accepted says, it refuses a pool that holds less than minLiquidity when it runs, and a pool that keeps no more observations than window has seconds (META, SGOV and GLD keep about 1,800 today: someone calls increaseObservationCardinalityNext(7200) on them first). poolInitCodeHash is the hash of Uniswap v3's pool creation code: the script stops at a pool whose address is not the one the factory's CREATE2 gives for it. SPY, TSLA, MSFT and META have thin v3 pools: whether the keeper is switched on for them is the admin's setAsset, not this file. watch is read by the keeper's dry run only (apps/keeper/src/watch.ts), never by the deploy script: the session in seconds after midnight UTC (15:00 to 19:45), the distance a price may be from the pool's average, and the greatest age of a Chainlink answer in seconds, as the design proposes them for the vault's own settings.",
